BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:"“Free Speech in Polarized Times" with Georgetown Law Professor 
 David Cole
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261019T153000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261019T170000
DTSTAMP:20261004T150559Z
UID:dartmouth-82747@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=82747\n\nDescription:\nFree speech is under assault.  Progressiv
 es question its value when weighed against egalitarian concerns.  The Whi
 te House has targeted universities\, lawyers\, and the press\, among other
 s\, for expressing views the administration disfavors.  And a majority of
  students report believing it’s justified to shout down speakers they do
 n’t like. Professor Cole\, who has been defending First Amendment right
 s for more than forty years\, including landmark cases in the Supreme Cour
 t\, will reflect on the essential character of free speech in our polarize
 d society.  His current client\, Mohsen Mahdawi\, a Columbia student faci
 ng deportation for his pro-Palestinian speech\, will follow with an accoun
 t of his own defense of First Amendment freedoms.\nDavid Cole is the Hon. 
 George J. Mitchell Professor in Law and Public Policy at Georgetown Univer
 sity Law Center and former National Legal Director of the ACLU.  He write
 s regularly for the New York Review of Books and is legal affairs correspo
 ndent for The Nation. He is the author or editor of ten books\, including 
 No Equal Justice: Race and Class in the American Criminal Justice System\,
  and Engines of Liberty: How Citizen Movements Succeed.\nDavid has litigat
 ed many pathbreaking cases in the Supreme Court\, including Texas v. John
 son\, which extended First Amendment protection to flag burning\; Bostock
  v. Clayton County\, which established that Title VII bans discrimination 
 on the basis of transgender status and sexual orientation\; and National R
 ifle Association v. Vullo\, which held that government officials cannot us
 e their regulatory authority to coerce private parties into blacklisting a
  disfavored political organization.  He has received many awards for his 
 civil liberties work.\n\nSponsor / organizer: Ethics Institute\, Governmen
 t Department\, Neukom Institute\, Philosophy Department\, Wright Center\n\
 nAudience: Public\n\nSource: Dartmouth\n\nWhy this was included: Free Food
  category\n\nFood availability is inferred from the source listing\; verif
 y details before attending.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261004T150559Z
LOCATION:Filene Auditorium\, Moore Building
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://home.dartmouth.edu/events/event?event=82747
X-MICROSOFT-CDO-BUSYSTATUS:FREE
END:VEVENT
